WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:05.920
Alright, so what is a row number?

00:05.960 --> 00:11.320
In SQL, the row number function going to go and assign for each row a unique number as a rank, and

00:11.320 --> 00:13.800
it doesn't care at all about the ties.

00:14.000 --> 00:19.600
That means if you have two rows sharing the same value, they will not share the same rank.

00:19.720 --> 00:21.280
Okay, so now we have very simple example.

00:21.280 --> 00:25.160
We have a list of all sales and we have the following query.

00:25.440 --> 00:28.320
So it's going to start with the ranking function or row number.

00:28.360 --> 00:30.760
It doesn't accept any argument inside it.

00:30.760 --> 00:32.920
And the definition of the window is going to be like this.

00:33.200 --> 00:35.440
Order by sales desc.

00:35.440 --> 00:40.640
So that means we're going to go and sort the data descending from the highest to the lowest.

00:40.640 --> 00:42.600
So SQL is going to go and do the following.

00:42.800 --> 00:45.000
The high is going to be the 100.

00:45.040 --> 00:47.200
The lowest is going to be the 20.

00:47.440 --> 00:49.400
And here we have twice the 80.

00:49.440 --> 00:52.760
So now once SQL done sorting the data what's going to happen.

00:52.760 --> 00:54.560
It's going to start assigning a rank.

00:54.720 --> 00:58.920
So the row number is going to go and assign a unique number for each row.

00:59.040 --> 01:01.000
So that means it's going to start with the first one.

01:01.040 --> 01:03.280
The 100 is going to be the rank number one.

01:03.320 --> 01:05.680
The next one is going to be ranked number two.

01:05.720 --> 01:11.210
The 80 is going to be ranked number three and the 54, and then the last one going to be five.

01:11.250 --> 01:14.690
And now if you check the output, you can see that all those numbers are unique.

01:14.690 --> 01:16.490
We don't have any repetitions.

01:16.490 --> 01:19.050
So 12345 there is no repetitions.

01:19.050 --> 01:20.930
They are unique distinct value.

01:21.170 --> 01:24.250
And as well there are no skipping of ranking.

01:24.250 --> 01:26.170
So that means we have here 123.

01:26.210 --> 01:28.610
There is no jumping to 6 or 7 or something.

01:28.650 --> 01:32.890
There are clear sequence of distinct value and there are no gaps.

01:32.890 --> 01:35.050
But still there is something special in our data.

01:35.050 --> 01:38.730
We can see that in the sales we have the same value twice.

01:38.730 --> 01:41.250
So we have two rows with the same sales.

01:41.410 --> 01:48.010
As you can see in the row number, they will get distinct value, so they will not share the same ranking.

01:48.010 --> 01:51.490
So that means row number does not handle the ties.

01:51.610 --> 01:56.850
If you have multiple rows sharing the same values, they will not share the same rank.

01:56.890 --> 01:59.850
They're going to have a distinct rank different ranks.

01:59.850 --> 02:01.850
So this is how the row number works in SQL.

02:01.890 --> 02:04.130
It generates unique ranks for each row.

02:04.490 --> 02:08.610
It does not handle the ties and as well it doesn't leave any gaps.

02:08.610 --> 02:10.410
So there is no skipping of ranking.

02:10.410 --> 02:13.870
So now let's go to SQL in order to have few examples and use cases.

02:13.870 --> 02:14.190
All right.

02:14.190 --> 02:15.350
So now we have the following task.

02:15.350 --> 02:16.150
It's very simple.

02:16.310 --> 02:20.150
Rank the orders based on their sales from the highest to the lowest.

02:20.150 --> 02:21.390
So now this is very easy.

02:21.430 --> 02:23.670
We're going to go and select first the data.

02:23.950 --> 02:26.110
So order ID product id.

02:26.150 --> 02:29.190
Let's take the sales as well and select the table.

02:29.190 --> 02:31.230
So it's going to be sales orders.

02:31.430 --> 02:33.030
Let's go and execute it.

02:33.030 --> 02:34.950
So thus we got all our orders.

02:34.950 --> 02:38.110
What we're going to do now is to assign for each row a rank.

02:38.110 --> 02:42.430
So that means we need a column here that contains the rank for each row.

02:42.550 --> 02:46.510
So in order to do that we're going to go and use the window function row number.

02:46.550 --> 02:49.950
It doesn't accept any argument inside it so it should be empty.

02:49.950 --> 02:51.830
And then we have to define the window.

02:51.830 --> 02:54.950
So as we learned the ranking functions we cannot leave it empty.

02:54.990 --> 02:57.230
We have to sort the data using order by.

02:57.270 --> 02:59.110
So order by is a must.

02:59.150 --> 03:00.950
We don't have to use any partition by.

03:00.990 --> 03:03.830
So we're going to rank all the data that we have inside the table.

03:03.830 --> 03:04.990
So how to sort the data.

03:04.990 --> 03:08.270
It says it should be based on their sales from highest to lowest.

03:08.310 --> 03:13.470
That means we order by sales since from highest to lowest we have to use the descending.

03:13.510 --> 03:17.950
And now we're going to go and give it a name sales rank and let's say row.

03:17.950 --> 03:20.040
Since we are using the row number.

03:20.040 --> 03:21.160
So that's it.

03:21.160 --> 03:21.880
It's very simple.

03:21.880 --> 03:23.200
Let's go and execute it.

03:23.200 --> 03:25.080
So now let's have a look to the results.

03:25.080 --> 03:30.280
Before SQL did sort the data by the order ID since we didn't define anything, but since now we are

03:30.320 --> 03:36.760
ordered by sale descending SQL went and sorted the data by the sales from the highest to the lowest

03:36.760 --> 03:41.720
and start assigning a rank or let's say an integer unique integer for each row.

03:41.720 --> 03:44.040
So now the highest order is going to be the order number eight.

03:44.080 --> 03:45.440
We have the sales of 90.

03:45.480 --> 03:46.640
This is the highest one.

03:46.640 --> 03:49.960
So as you can see we have one, two, three, four, five until ten.

03:49.960 --> 03:54.200
So now by checking the result you can see that the ranking here is unique.

03:54.200 --> 03:56.240
So there is no duplicates over here.

03:56.360 --> 03:58.760
And as well there is no skipping or gaps.

03:58.760 --> 04:01.000
So we have everything between 1 and 10.

04:01.040 --> 04:05.080
Even though that we have in our data a couple of sales that sharing the same value.

04:05.120 --> 04:07.240
So for example we have those two orders.

04:07.240 --> 04:12.040
You can see both of them has the 60 at the sales, but they don't share the same ranking.

04:12.080 --> 04:12.480
Right.

04:12.560 --> 04:14.600
So we have here as well the nine and three.

04:14.640 --> 04:18.720
They share the same value 20 but they don't share the same ranking.

04:18.720 --> 04:20.240
So with that we have solved the task.

04:20.240 --> 04:21.080
It's very simple.

04:21.080 --> 04:24.840
We have now a rank based on the sales from highest to the lowest.
